</worksheet>
</subsection>

      <subsection xml:id="sec-04-05-distance-two-points">
        <title>Find the Distance Between Two Points in Space</title>
      <p>
We often need a formula for measuring the distance between two points in
three-dimensional space.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-distance-between-two-points-3d">
  <title>Distance Between Two Points in Space</title>

  <p>
    For two points <m>P_1 (x_1, y_1, z_1)</m> and <m>P_2 (x_2, y_2, z_2)</m>,
    the distance between them is given by
  </p>

  <me>
    d = \sqrt{ ( x_2 - x_1 )^2 + ( y_2 - y_1 )^2 + ( z_2 - z_1 )^2 }.
  </me>

  <p>
    This formula can be used to describe spheres in three-dimensional space.
    A sphere of radius <m>r</m> centered at <m>(a,b,c)</m> has equation
  </p>

  <me>
    r^2 = ( x - a )^2 + ( y - b )^2 + ( z - c )^2.
  </me>

  <p>
    In particular, the equation
  </p>

  <me>
    r^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2
  </me>

  <p>
    represents a sphere of radius <m>r</m> centered at the origin.
  </p>
</definition>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-distance-between-points-3d">
  <title>In Class Example</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-distance-between-points-3d">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the distance between the points
        <m>P_1 = (-2, 1, 5)</m> and <m>P_2 = (3, -1, 2)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Use the distance formula for points in three-dimensional space.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>

       </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-01-xx-3d-coordinates-practice">
  <title>Exercises on Points in Three-Dimensional Space</title>

  <worksheet xml:id="points-3d-practice">
    <title>Practice Problems</title>

    <exercise xml:id="points-3d-practice-1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Plot the following point in three-dimensional space.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(-2,-3,-2)</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

<image source="images/ch01/ThreeDCoordinateGrid.png">
          <description>
          A three-dimensional coordinate system is shown with horizontal, vertical, and depth axes labeled x, y, and z.
          A straight line passes through the origin and extends diagonally across the coordinate space.  
          </description>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="points-3d-practice-2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the distance <m>r</m> between the origin and the point
          <m>P=(8,0,-6)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="points-3d-practice-3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without plotting, identify the octant or coordinate plane for each point.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p><m>(3,-2,5)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(0,-4,1)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(-1,-2,-3)</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="points-3d-practice-4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          True or False. Justify each answer.
        </p>

        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Any point with a zero coordinate lies in exactly one octant.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The point <m>(-2,5,0)</m> lies on the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Points in Octant III must have all coordinates negative.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The distance between two points in space can never be less than
              the distance between their projections in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </worksheet>
</subsection>

    </section>

